---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Project Setup - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="project-setup">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="Instead of reading every doc yourself, you can ask the agent how to get an unfamiliar project running and have it walk you through the steps.">
			You've joined the Kaiju Mutual web team. The setup instructions are spread across several docs and config files, and the
			README only says "Not sure how to get started? Ask your agent."
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message
			kind="email"
			subject="Welcome to the team"
			from="Engineering Lead"
			to="You"
			date="Thu, 1 Oct 2026 09:05"
			attachments={[{ name: "kaiju-mutual.zip", href: "/downloads/kaiju-mutual.zip" }]}
		>
			<p>Welcome aboard! The codebase is attached. Unzip it, open your agent in the folder, and get everything running locally.</p>
			<p>
				Make sure the portals show live kaiju watch data, not just the sample data. Tidewatch, our monitoring vendor, is a
				separate service, but the API needs to reach it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Make sure you setup herdr and agent-browser as tools in your agent, they will be super helpful for you to use.
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent work out how to install and run Kaiju Mutual, then walk you through it.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Find the setup steps across the docs and put them in order.",
				"Tell you which tools to install, and check which ones you already have.",
				"Have you start herdr and split it into two panes: the agent in one, the servers in the other.",
				"Make sure agent-browser is installed, so the agent can check the apps in a real browser.",
				"Install the dependencies and set up the local database with sample data.",
				"Connect the API to Tidewatch so the portals show live kaiju watch data.",
				"Start the servers in their pane, then read that pane's log for warnings.",
				"Give you the URL for each app.",
			]}
		>
			<figure class="mt-5">
				<a href="/images/challenges/1.herdr-workspace.png" target="_blank">
					<img
						src="/images/challenges/1.herdr-workspace.png"
						alt="herdr with two panes: Claude Code on top, and the dev servers below showing the API log and the local URL for each app"
						class="w-full rounded border border-stone-200"
						loading="lazy"
					/>
				</a>
				<figcaption class="mt-2 font-sans text-[12px] text-stone-500">
					The finished layout: the agent on top, and the servers below, where the agent can read their log.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</Checklist>

		<Callout variant="warning" title="Read before you run">
			<p>
				Setup steps often pipe a script from the internet into your shell. Read each install command the agent proposes before
				you approve it, and prefer a package manager such as Homebrew when the docs offer one.
			</p>
		</Callout>

		<Answer
			prompt={`How do I get this project running? Read the docs and config files, tell me what I need to install and check what's already on my machine. The email lists herdr and agent-browser as tools the team uses, so include those too. Then walk me through each step in order, including how to lay out my workspace in herdr.

Have me start the servers then you can check everything is running correctly.`}
		>
			<p>
				No single file has the whole answer. The agent has to piece it together from the{" "}
				<code>docs/training</code> folder, the root and API <code>package.json</code> files, and the API's{" "}
				<code>.env.example</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The step people miss is copying <code>apps/api/.env.example</code> to <code>apps/api/.env</code>. Without it every app
				still loads, and the only sign is a warning in the API log that the Tidewatch sync was skipped. An agent that checks the
				log after starting the servers catches it.
			</p>
			<p>
				A <code>tidewatch row skipped</code> warning for an unknown district code is expected on a healthy start. If the agent
				starts fixing it, steer it back to the task.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
